F011 live Supabase verification (Management API + admin SDK probes)
Project ref: femrzpfslejzqnvfsfoe
Date: 2026-07-17

1) Auth config preflight (GET /v1/projects/{ref}/config/auth)
   MAILER_AUTOCONFIRM: false            -> email confirmation IS required (matches AS-009 / discovery Q4=a)
   disable_signup: false
   external_email_enabled: true
   external_google_enabled: true        -> pre-existing, not touched by F011 (Google OAuth is a separate feature)
   site_url: http://localhost:3000
   password_min_length: 6
   rate_limit_email_sent: 2             -> confirms mission notes: ~2/h built-in email send limit
   uri_allow_list (before): ""

2) Config change applied (PATCH /v1/projects/{ref}/config/auth)
   uri_allow_list -> "http://localhost:3000/**"
   Reason: without an allow-list entry covering /auth/callback, Supabase would
   reject the emailRedirectTo used by signUp()/resend() at send time. site_url
   alone is not guaranteed to match subpaths; added explicitly. Verified via
   follow-up GET: uri_allow_list == "http://localhost:3000/**".

3) Behavioral probe: signInWithPassword against an admin-created UNCONFIRMED
   user (email_confirm: false) -- proves Supabase itself refuses to establish
   a session for an unconfirmed user (AS-009's actual enforcement point):
     signIn error: { message: 'Email not confirmed', status: 400, code: 'email_not_confirmed' }
     signIn data.session present: false
     signIn data.user present: false

4) Behavioral probe: signInWithPassword with a WRONG password for an existing
   user, and with a NONEXISTENT email -- both return the identical error
   (AS-017, no user-enumeration leak already at the Supabase layer; our
   mapping preserves this):
     wrongPw error:     { message: 'Invalid login credentials', status: 400, code: 'invalid_credentials' }
     nonexistent error: { message: 'Invalid login credentials', status: 400, code: 'invalid_credentials' }

5) Migration supabase/migrations/0002_profiles_on_signup.sql applied live via
   POST /v1/projects/{ref}/database/query -> HTTP 201.
   Verified via follow-up SELECTs:
     trigger_exists:   [{"tgname":"on_auth_user_created","tgenabled":"O"}]
     function_secdef:  [{"proname":"handle_new_auth_user","prosecdef":true}]

6) Behavioral proof the trigger fires: admin.auth.admin.createUser() for a
   fresh throwaway email, then immediately SELECT public.profiles for that
   user_id with the admin client:
     profile row via trigger: { user_id: '<new-user-id>', onboarded_at: null, is_admin: false }
     error: null
   Test user deleted immediately after (auth.users cascade removes the
   profiles row too, per 0001_profiles.sql's ON DELETE CASCADE).

Note: real auth.signUp() calls against example.com-style throwaway emails hit
the project's own ~2/h email-send rate limit almost immediately during this
probing (one attempt returned "Email address ... is invalid", subsequent
attempts within the same minute returned "email rate limit exceeded"). This
confirms the mission notes' warning and is why the committed test suite
(src/app/(auth)/__tests__/auth-flow.integration.test.ts) uses
admin.auth.admin.createUser() (which never sends email) for AS-008/AS-009/
AS-017 assertions instead of looping real signUp() calls, exactly as this
feature's run instructions direct ("do NOT depend on actually receiving an
email in tests, use the admin API to set/read confirmation state").
